  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 154 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAside from the chance (20-40%) of light showers continuing into \nSunday morning, next week will start off dry. By Tuesday, a longwave \ntrough is forecast to be swinging through the Intermountain West. \nDiscrepancies in guidance begin to build at this point in time, such \nas the amount of vorticity lobes that are ejected out ahead of the \nsystem and into the Mid-Mississippi Valley as well as the timing and \nstrength of warm air and moisture advection associated with it. \nDespite this, ensembles are very optimistic regarding our rainfall \nchances. Both the NBM and LREF 24 hr probabilities for measurable \nprecipitation between Tuesday and Wednesday reach 90% in portions of \nthe area. Although this is washed out given the previously discussed \ndiscrepancies and low model resolution, it does increase confidence \nin widespread rain falling during the middle of next week.\n\nOtherwise, the temperature forecast appears to be fairly steady \nthrough next week as warm air advection and cloud cover negate each \nother. Highs in the low 70s to low 80s are forecast each day with a \nnorth-south gradient.",
